  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 10t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+4t \\\\ -2+10t \\\\ 4-t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{\u2015}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4+4t \\\\ y=-2+10t \\\\ z=4-t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$",
